Yes. The common pattern is many local strands becoming a higher-order structure whose behavior cannot be read from any strand alone.

Rope is the simplest version: fibers → yarns → strands → rope. Strength is not just “more fiber”; it comes from twist geometry, load sharing, friction, redundancy, and failure distribution.

Chain mail is different. It is not primarily braided continuity but interlocking topology. Each ring is weak in isolation, yet the mesh redistributes force laterally. The key abstraction becomes:

\text{local linkage} \rightarrow \text{global resilience}

DNA is richer still because the braid is also code, memory, executable instruction, error-corrected inheritance, and a generator of generators. DNA does not directly “contain a body” in the simple sense. It participates in regulatory networks, cellular machinery, developmental gradients, environmental feedback, and timing. Its expression is conditional.

So one abstract object can unfold through multiple planes:

\text{sequence}
\rightarrow
\text{structure}
\rightarrow
\text{regulation}
\rightarrow
\text{expression}
\rightarrow
\text{phenotype}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{environmental consequence}

Then those consequences loop back onto selection, regulation, and future expression.

What you’re calling the shadow plane can be treated rigorously as a projection space: the lower-dimensional representation where only some properties of the higher-order object remain visible.

A 3-D knot casts a 2-D shadow.

The shadow preserves some things:

* adjacency,
* crossings,
* rough geometry.

But it loses others:

* which strand is actually above another unless encoded,
* true depth,
* tension,
* material properties.

Likewise, a social graph is a shadow of actual human relationships. A balance sheet is a shadow of a company. A genome sequence is a shadow of a living developmental process. A map is a shadow of territory. A story is a shadow of lived events.

So the important rule is:

\text{projection} = \text{preserved invariants} + \text{discarded dimensions}

And yes: higher-order structures require something of their lower-order projections if they are to remain recoverable.

Not everything must survive. But enough invariants must.

If you project a cube into two dimensions, different 3-D objects can produce the same 2-D silhouette. Reconstruction becomes ambiguous. You need more projections, extra constraints, or prior knowledge.

That is exactly what tomography does: many shadows let you infer the hidden object.

And the same abstraction applies elsewhere:

One behavior does not identify a person.
One metric does not identify a business.
One sentence does not identify a belief system.
One phenotype does not identify an entire regulatory architecture.

But multiple projections intersecting can constrain the hidden state.

P_1(H), P_2(H), P_3(H), \ldots
\Rightarrow
\text{increasingly constrained estimate of }H

where H is the higher-order hidden structure.

Then there is another beautiful transition: projections can start interacting with each other, even when the originating object is elsewhere.

A reputation is a projection of a person.

A credit score is another projection.

A résumé is another.

Those projections then interact in institutional space and produce very real consequences for the person.

So:

H \rightarrow P

eventually becomes

P \rightarrow \text{world} \rightarrow H

The shadow starts acting back on the thing that cast it.

That is why models, identities, rankings, narratives, symbols, laws, money, and algorithms can become causally powerful. They are initially representations, but once agents coordinate around them, they become operative structures.

And now rope, chain mail, DNA, institutions, narratives, and cognition fit under one larger morphology:

Composition
small units combine.

Coupling
units constrain one another.

Emergence
the composite gains new capacities.

Projection
the composite appears differently at different observational levels.

Compression
some dimensions disappear from representation.

Reconstruction
multiple projections can recover hidden structure.

Reflexivity
projections can alter the thing they represent.

Inheritance
successful structure gets copied into new substrates.

That’s a pretty deep reusable primitive:

braid → structure → projection → interaction of projections → feedback into source

And once you see that, “higher” and “lower” stop simply meaning better and worse. They mean different resolution spaces with different invariants and different laws of useful description.